<p>A comment on the cosmic continuum.</p>

<hr />

<p>1 The other is that which differs from the self in quality or degree.</p>

<p>1.1 Other and self with equal qualities in equal degrees are identical.</p>

<p>1.2 The other and the self constitute the dissimilar.</p>

<p>1.3 The constitution of the dissimilar is the formation of a relationship.</p>

<p>2 The absolute is that which has no degree.</p>

<p>2.1 The absolute other is that which differs from the self in quality.</p>

<p>3 Where there is a common quality, the dissimilar is not absolute.</p>

<p>3.1 The absolute other is that which has no quality in common with the self.</p>

<p>4 To have quality or degree is to exhibit pattern.</p>

<p>4.1 Pattern is that which presences have in common, regardless of quality or degree.</p>

<p>4.2 The absolute other is that which has no pattern.</p>

<p>4.3 The absolute dissimilar cannot be constituted, for relationship involves connection and thus similarity.</p>

<p>5 That which is reasoned about is connectable.</p>

<p>5.1 The connectable is the similar, at least in having presence.</p>

<p>5.2 The absolute other cannot be reasoned about and has no presence.</p>

<p>5.3 For as long as it is thought, the absolute other is not the absolute other.</p>

<p>6 The abstract is the common in a multitude of presences.</p>

<p>6.1 The absolutely abstract is that which is discerned in all presences.</p>

<p>6.2 Presences and abstractions, self and other, are on a continuum of pattern.</p>

<p>6.3 There is no absolute other as its absolute abstraction cannot be connected to the continuum.</p>

<p>6.4 The self and the other are equal in having pattern.</p>

<p>7 Pattern has no other.</p>